Both linear coefficients are divisible by \(6\). Define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:n-def}
 n=n(k)=ak+b,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{description}[leftmargin=3.2em,style=nextline]
\item[$a=L_1/6$] \bignum{5103243448423190660018404944928000789930010683701564743465}
\item[$b=L_0/6$] \bignum{1820733127217158956577191662349053768348092988705876831189}
\end{description}
Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:linear-compress}
 L_1k+L_0=6n(k).
\end{equation}
A direct coefficient comparison gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:coefficient-check}
 C_3=36a^3,\qquad C_2=108a^2b,\qquad C_1=108ab^2,\qquad C_0=36b^3-19.
\end{equation}
Indeed,
\[
36(ak+b)^3-19
=36a^3k^3+108a^2bk^2+108ab^2k+(36b^3-19).
\]
Therefore the whole numerator is exactly
\begin{equation}\label{eq:P-identity}
 P(k)=36n(k)^3-19.
\end{equation}

Now put
\begin{equation}\label{eq:q-def}
 q=12x+7.
\end{equation}
Using \eqref{eq:linear-compress} and \eqref{eq:P-identity}, equation \eqref{eq:original} becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:compressed}
 y^2=(q+6n)^2+\frac{36n^3-19}{q},
 \qquad n=n(k),\quad q=12x+7.
\end{equation}
The denominator condition is \(q\neq0\), equivalently \(12x+7\neq0\).

\section*{2. Derivation of the Weierstrass curve}

Let
\begin{equation}\label{eq:R-def}
 R=36n^3-19.
\end{equation}
Then \eqref{eq:compressed} is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:qR-form}
 y^2=(q+6n)^2+\frac{R}{q}.
\end{equation}
Define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:UY-def}
 U=\frac{R}{q},
 \qquad
 Y=Uy=\frac{Ry}{q}.
\end{equation}
Since \(q=R/U\), equation \eqref{eq:qR-form} gives
\begin{align*}
Y^2
&=U^2y^2\\
&=U^2\left(\left(\frac{R}{U}+6n\right)^2+U\right)\\
&=(R+6nU)^2+U^3\\
&=U^3+36n^2U^2+12nRU+R^2.
\end{align*}
Thus the intermediate cubic is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:U-curve}
 Y^2=U^3+36n^2U^2+12nRU+R^2.
\end{equation}
Remove the quadratic term by shifting
\begin{equation}\label{eq:X-shift}
 X=U+12n^2,
 \qquad
 U=X-12n^2.
\end{equation}
Substituting \eqref{eq:X-shift} into \eqref{eq:U-curve} gives
\begin{align*}
Y^2
&=(X-12n^2)^3+36n^2(X-12n^2)^2\\
&\qquad +12nR(X-12n^2)+R^2.
\end{align*}
Using \(R=36n^3-19\), the \(X^2\)-coefficient cancels. The coefficient of \(X\) is
\[
12nR-432n^4
=12n(36n^3-19)-432n^4
=-228n.
\]
The constant term is
\begin{align*}
3456n^6-144n^3R+R^2
&=3456n^6-144n^3(36n^3-19)+(36n^3-19)^2\\
&=-432n^6+1368n^3+361.
\end{align*}
Therefore the associated short Weierstrass equation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:curve-n}
\boxed{Y^2=X^3-228nX-432n^6+1368n^3+361.}
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:curve-n-alt}
\boxed{Y^2=X^3-228nX-\left(432n^6-1368n^3-361\right).}
\end{equation}

\section*{3. Final curve attached to the parameter \(k\)}

Substituting \(n=n(k)=ak+b\) into \eqref{eq:curve-n}, the curve attached to the original equation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:curve-k}
\boxed{
E_k:\quad
Y^2=X^3-228n(k)X-432n(k)^6+1368n(k)^3+361.
}
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:curve-k-alt}
\boxed{
E_k:\quad
Y^2=X^3-228n(k)X-\left(432n(k)^6-1368n(k)^3-361\right).
}
\end{equation}
Here
\[
n(k)=ak+b,
\]
with \(a\) and \(b\) given in Section 1.

This is the natural Mordell-type elliptic curve in short Weierstrass form. Strictly speaking, it is not a pure Mordell curve of the special form \(Y^2=X^3+D\), because the linear term \(-228n(k)X\) is present.

\section*{4. Birational correspondence}

The forward change of variables from the original equation to the Weierstrass equation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:forward}
\boxed{
X=12n^2+\frac{36n^3-19}{12x+7},
\qquad
Y=\frac{(36n^3-19)y}{12x+7}.
}
\end{equation}
Conversely, away from the exceptional denominator \(X=12n^2\), one recovers
\begin{equation}\label{eq:inverse}
\boxed{
12x+7=\frac{36n^3-19}{X-12n^2},
\qquad
 y=\frac{Y}{X-12n^2}.
}
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:inverse-x}
\boxed{
 x=\frac{1}{12}\left(\frac{36n^3-19}{X-12n^2}-7\right),
\qquad
 y=\frac{Y}{X-12n^2}.
}
\end{equation}
Thus rational points on \(E_k\) with \(X\ne12n(k)^2\) correspond rationally to solutions of the original equation, subject to \(12x+7\ne0\).

For integer solutions in the original variables, the inverse map must additionally produce integral values. In particular,
\[
q=\frac{36n^3-19}{X-12n^2}
\]
must be an integer satisfying
\[
q\equiv7\pmod{12},
\]
and \(Y/(X-12n^2)\) must be an integer.

\section*{5. Nonsingularity}

For a short Weierstrass curve
\[
Y^2=X^3+\mathcal A X+\mathcal B,
\]
we have
\[
\mathcal A=-228n,
\qquad
\mathcal B=-432n^6+1368n^3+361.
\]
A direct factorization gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:disc-factor}
4\mathcal A^3+27\mathcal B^2
=27(4n^3-19)(36n^3-19)^3.
\end{equation}
Therefore the discriminant is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:discriminant}
\Delta=-16(4\mathcal A^3+27\mathcal B^2)
=-432(4n^3-19)(36n^3-19)^3.
\end{equation}
For every integer \(n\), this is nonzero, since neither \(4n^3=19\) nor \(36n^3=19\) has an integer solution. Hence for integer \(k\), the curve \(E_k\) is nonsingular over \(\QQ\).

\section*{6. Summary}

The huge equation reduces to
\[
y^2=(q+6n)^2+\frac{36n^3-19}{q},
\qquad
q=12x+7,
\qquad
n=n(k)=ak+b.
\]
The associated curve is
\[
\boxed{Y^2=X^3-228nX-432n^6+1368n^3+361,}
\]
where \(n=n(k)\). Equivalently,
\[
\boxed{E_k:\ Y^2=X^3-228n(k)X-\left(432n(k)^6-1368n(k)^3-361\right).}
\]
The explicit correspondence is
\[
X=12n^2+\frac{36n^3-19}{12x+7},
\qquad
Y=\frac{(36n^3-19)y}{12x+7}.
\]

